{"id":"frq-glucose-meal-model","type":"model","typeName":"Analyze Model or Visual Representation","points":4,"title":"Blood glucose and hormone levels after a meal","units":["unit-4"],"topics":["feedback"],"practices":["2.A","2.B","2.C","6.E"],"stimulus":{"kind":"model","title":"A model of glucose control, and one meal","html":"<div class=\"bio-stim-text\"><p>The diagram is a model of how blood glucose is regulated. The graph shows blood glucose (mg/dL), insulin (µU/mL) and glucagon (pg/mL) in a healthy teenager during the 3 hours after a meal rich in carbohydrate, eaten at time 0. Each is plotted in its own unit.</p></div><div class=\"bio-chart\"><svg class=\"bio-svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 560 320\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"A model of glucose control, and one meal: line graph of Level (unit in the key) against Time after the meal for Glucose (mg/dL), Insulin (µU/mL), Glucagon (pg/mL). The data table follows.\"><line class=\"axis\" x1=\"64\" y1=\"262\" x2=\"542\" y2=\"262\"/><line class=\"axis\" x1=\"64\" y1=\"18\" x2=\"64\" y2=\"262\"/><line class=\"grid\" x1=\"64\" y1=\"262.0\" x2=\"542\" y2=\"262.0\"/><text class=\"tick\" x=\"56\" y=\"266.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">0</text><line class=\"grid\" x1=\"64\" y1=\"231.5\" x2=\"542\" y2=\"231.5\"/><text class=\"tick\" x=\"56\" y=\"235.5\" text-anchor=\"end\">20</text><line class=\"grid\" x1=\"64\" y1=\"201.0\" x2=\"542\" y2=\"201.0\"/><text class=\"tick\" x=\"56\" y=\"205.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">40</text><line class=\"grid\" x1=\"64\" y1=\"170.5\" x2=\"542\" y2=\"170.5\"/><text class=\"tick\" x=\"56\" y=\"174.5\" text-anchor=\"end\">60</text><line class=\"grid\" x1=\"64\" y1=\"140.0\" x2=\"542\" y2=\"140.0\"/><text class=\"tick\" x=\"56\" y=\"144.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">80</text><line class=\"grid\" x1=\"64\" y1=\"109.5\" x2=\"542\" y2=\"109.5\"/><text class=\"tick\" x=\"56\" y=\"113.5\" text-anchor=\"end\">100</text><line class=\"grid\" x1=\"64\" y1=\"79.0\" x2=\"542\" y2=\"79.0\"/><text class=\"tick\" x=\"56\" y=\"83.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">120</text><line class=\"grid\" x1=\"64\" y1=\"48.5\" x2=\"542\" y2=\"48.5\"/><text class=\"tick\" x=\"56\" y=\"52.5\" text-anchor=\"end\">140</text><line class=\"grid\" x1=\"64\" y1=\"18.0\" x2=\"542\" y2=\"18.0\"/><text class=\"tick\" x=\"56\" y=\"22.0\" text-anchor=\"end\">160</text><text class=\"tick\" x=\"64.0\" y=\"280\" text-anchor=\"middle\">0</text><text class=\"tick\" x=\"143.7\" y=\"280\" text-anchor=\"middle\">30</text><text class=\"tick\" x=\"223.3\" y=\"280\" text-anchor=\"middle\">60</text><text class=\"tick\" x=\"303.0\" y=\"280\" text-anchor=\"middle\">90</text><text class=\"tick\" x=\"382.7\" y=\"280\" text-anchor=\"middle\">120</text><text class=\"tick\" x=\"462.3\" y=\"280\" text-anchor=\"middle\">150</text><text class=\"tick\" x=\"542.0\" y=\"280\" text-anchor=\"middle\">180</text><polyline class=\"series s1\" points=\"64.0,132.4 103.8,94.3 143.7,56.1 183.5,48.5 223.3,63.8 303.0,94.3 382.7,117.1 462.3,127.8 542.0,132.4\"/><circle class=\"pt s1\" cx=\"64.0\" cy=\"132.4\" r=\"4.5\"/><circle class=\"pt s1\" cx=\"103.8\" cy=\"94.3\" r=\"4.5\"/><circle class=\"pt s1\" cx=\"143.7\" cy=\"56.1\" r=\"4.5\"/><circle class=\"pt s1\" cx=\"183.5\" cy=\"48.5\" r=\"4.5\"/><circle class=\"pt s1\" cx=\"223.3\" cy=\"63.8\" r=\"4.5\"/><circle class=\"pt s1\" cx=\"303.0\" cy=\"94.3\" r=\"4.5\"/><circle class=\"pt s1\" cx=\"382.7\" cy=\"117.1\" r=\"4.5\"/><circle class=\"pt s1\" cx=\"462.3\" cy=\"127.8\" r=\"4.5\"/><circle class=\"pt s1\" cx=\"542.0\" cy=\"132.4\" r=\"4.5\"/><polyline class=\"series s2\" points=\"64.0,249.8 103.8,223.9 143.7,185.8 183.5,167.4 223.3,173.6 303.0,201.0 382.7,228.4 462.3,243.7 542.0,248.3\"/><rect class=\"pt s2\" x=\"60.0\" y=\"245.8\" width=\"8\" height=\"8\"/><rect class=\"pt s2\" x=\"99.8\" y=\"219.9\" width=\"8\" height=\"8\"/><rect class=\"pt s2\" x=\"139.7\" y=\"181.8\" width=\"8\" height=\"8\"/><rect class=\"pt s2\" x=\"179.5\" y=\"163.4\" width=\"8\" height=\"8\"/><rect class=\"pt s2\" x=\"219.3\" y=\"169.6\" width=\"8\" height=\"8\"/><rect class=\"pt s2\" x=\"299.0\" y=\"197.0\" width=\"8\" height=\"8\"/><rect class=\"pt s2\" x=\"378.7\" y=\"224.4\" width=\"8\" height=\"8\"/><rect class=\"pt s2\" x=\"458.3\" y=\"239.7\" width=\"8\" height=\"8\"/><rect class=\"pt s2\" x=\"538.0\" y=\"244.3\" width=\"8\" height=\"8\"/><polyline class=\"series s3\" points=\"64.0,124.8 103.8,133.9 143.7,143.1 183.5,152.2 223.3,155.3 303.0,149.1 382.7,137.0 462.3,127.8 542.0,124.8\"/><circle class=\"pt s3\" cx=\"64.0\" cy=\"124.8\" r=\"4.5\"/><circle class=\"pt s3\" cx=\"103.8\" cy=\"133.9\" r=\"4.5\"/><circle class=\"pt s3\" cx=\"143.7\" cy=\"143.1\" r=\"4.5\"/><circle class=\"pt s3\" cx=\"183.5\" cy=\"152.2\" r=\"4.5\"/><circle class=\"pt s3\" cx=\"223.3\" cy=\"155.3\" r=\"4.5\"/><circle class=\"pt s3\" cx=\"303.0\" cy=\"149.1\" r=\"4.5\"/><circle class=\"pt s3\" cx=\"382.7\" cy=\"137.0\" r=\"4.5\"/><circle class=\"pt s3\" cx=\"462.3\" cy=\"127.8\" r=\"4.5\"/><circle class=\"pt s3\" cx=\"542.0\" cy=\"124.8\" r=\"4.5\"/><text class=\"lbl\" x=\"303\" y=\"308\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Time after the meal (min)</text><text class=\"lbl\" transform=\"translate(16 140) rotate(-90)\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Level (unit in the key)</text></svg><p class=\"bio-legend\"><span class=\"s1\"><i aria-hidden=\"true\"></i>Glucose (mg/dL)</span><span class=\"s2\"><i aria-hidden=\"true\"></i>Insulin (µU/mL)</span><span class=\"s3\"><i aria-hidden=\"true\"></i>Glucagon (pg/mL)</span></p><details class=\"bio-chart-data\"><summary>Data table</summary><div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Data table\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Time after the meal (min)</th><th scope=\"col\">Glucose (mg/dL)</th><th scope=\"col\">Insulin (µU/mL)</th><th scope=\"col\">Glucagon (pg/mL)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\">0</th><td>85</td><td>8</td><td>90</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">15</th><td>110</td><td>25</td><td>84</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">30</th><td>135</td><td>50</td><td>78</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">45</th><td>140</td><td>62</td><td>72</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">60</th><td>130</td><td>58</td><td>70</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">90</th><td>110</td><td>40</td><td>74</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">120</th><td>95</td><td>22</td><td>82</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">150</th><td>88</td><td>12</td><td>88</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">180</th><td>85</td><td>9</td><td>90</td></tr></tbody></table></div></details></div><div class=\"bio-stim-html\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 760 400\" width=\"100%\" style=\"max-width:760px;height:auto\" role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"frqgl-t frqgl-d\"> <title id=\"frqgl-t\">Negative feedback loops that hold blood glucose near its set point</title> <desc id=\"frqgl-d\">A box on the left shows blood glucose near its set point, about 70 to 100 milligrams per deciliter when fasting. Top loop: after a meal glucose rises above the range; beta cells in the pancreas sense the rise and release insulin; muscle and fat cells take up glucose and the liver stores it as glycogen; glucose falls back to the range. Bottom loop: during fasting or exercise glucose falls below the range; alpha cells in the pancreas sense the fall and release glucagon; the liver breaks down glycogen and releases glucose; glucose rises back to the range. In each loop the response cancels the change that started it.</desc> <defs> <marker id=\"frqgl-a\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"8\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L10,5 L0,10 Z\" fill=\"#1F2A28\"/></marker> </defs> <rect width=\"760\" height=\"400\" fill=\"#ffffff\"/> <g font-family=\"Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif\" fill=\"#1F2A28\"> <!-- center --> <rect x=\"14\" y=\"150\" width=\"160\" height=\"100\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"#C4E8DC\" stroke=\"#009E73\" stroke-width=\"3\"/> <g font-size=\"13\" text-anchor=\"middle\"><text x=\"94\" y=\"178\" font-weight=\"700\">Blood glucose</text><text x=\"94\" y=\"196\">near its set point</text><text x=\"94\" y=\"214\">(about 70-100</text><text x=\"94\" y=\"232\">mg/dL fasting)</text></g> <!-- top row --> <g font-size=\"13\" text-anchor=\"middle\"> <rect x=\"200\" y=\"30\" width=\"160\" height=\"72\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#C3DDF0\" stroke=\"#0072B2\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/> <text x=\"280\" y=\"56\">Glucose rises</text><text x=\"280\" y=\"73\">above the range</text><text x=\"280\" y=\"90\">(after a meal)</text> <rect x=\"390\" y=\"30\" width=\"160\" height=\"72\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#C3DDF0\" stroke=\"#0072B2\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/> <text x=\"470\" y=\"56\">Beta cells in the</text><text x=\"470\" y=\"73\">pancreas sense it and</text><text x=\"470\" y=\"90\" font-weight=\"700\">release insulin</text> <rect x=\"580\" y=\"30\" width=\"170\" height=\"72\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#C3DDF0\" stroke=\"#0072B2\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/> <text x=\"665\" y=\"56\">Muscle and fat cells</text><text x=\"665\" y=\"73\">take up glucose; liver</text><text x=\"665\" y=\"90\">stores it as glycogen</text> </g> <g font-size=\"12\" font-style=\"italic\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#3D4A66\"><text x=\"280\" y=\"120\">stimulus</text><text x=\"470\" y=\"120\">sensor and control center</text><text x=\"665\" y=\"120\">effectors (response)</text></g> <!-- bottom row --> <g font-size=\"13\" text-anchor=\"middle\"> <rect x=\"200\" y=\"298\" width=\"160\" height=\"72\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#F8E3B8\" stroke=\"#E69F00\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/> <text x=\"280\" y=\"324\">Glucose falls</text><text x=\"280\" y=\"341\">below the range</text><text x=\"280\" y=\"358\">(fasting, exercise)</text> <rect x=\"390\" y=\"298\" width=\"160\" height=\"72\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#F8E3B8\" stroke=\"#E69F00\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/> <text x=\"470\" y=\"324\">Alpha cells in the</text><text x=\"470\" y=\"341\">pancreas sense it and</text><text x=\"470\" y=\"358\" font-weight=\"700\">release glucagon</text> <rect x=\"580\" y=\"298\" width=\"170\" height=\"72\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#F8E3B8\" stroke=\"#E69F00\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/> <text x=\"665\" y=\"324\">Liver breaks down</text><text x=\"665\" y=\"341\">glycogen and releases</text><text x=\"665\" y=\"358\">glucose into the blood</text> </g> <g font-size=\"12\" font-style=\"italic\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#3D4A66\"><text x=\"280\" y=\"290\">stimulus</text><text x=\"470\" y=\"290\">sensor and control center</text><text x=\"665\" y=\"290\">effector (response)</text></g> <!-- arrows --> <g stroke=\"#1F2A28\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" fill=\"none\" marker-end=\"url(#frqgl-a)\"> <path d=\"M94,148 L94,66 L196,66\"/> <path d=\"M94,252 L94,334 L196,334\"/> <line x1=\"362\" y1=\"66\" x2=\"386\" y2=\"66\"/><line x1=\"552\" y1=\"66\" x2=\"576\" y2=\"66\"/> <line x1=\"362\" y1=\"334\" x2=\"386\" y2=\"334\"/><line x1=\"552\" y1=\"334\" x2=\"576\" y2=\"334\"/> <path d=\"M738,104 C738,175 300,170 178,185\"/> <path d=\"M738,296 C738,225 300,230 178,215\"/> </g> <g font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" text-anchor=\"middle\"><text x=\"450\" y=\"160\" fill=\"#0072B2\">glucose falls back</text><text x=\"450\" y=\"252\" fill=\"#B35F00\">glucose rises back</text></g> </g> </svg></div>"},"parts":[{"label":"a","prompt":"<b>Describe</b> the role of the beta cells in the model.","points":1,"rubric":[{"point":"Describes the beta cells as the sensor and control center: they detect the rise in blood glucose and respond by releasing insulin.","accept":["they sense high blood glucose and release insulin","beta cells detect the stimulus and send the insulin signal","sensor and control center that secretes insulin when glucose rises"]}],"sample":"The beta cells act as the sensor and control center of the upper loop. They detect that blood glucose has risen above its normal range and respond by releasing insulin into the blood."},{"label":"b","prompt":"Using the graph, <b>explain</b> how the data show that glucose is regulated by negative feedback.","points":1,"rubric":[{"point":"Explains that insulin rises as glucose rises (peaking at about 45 minutes) and glucose then falls back to about 85 mg/dL, after which insulin falls too: the response opposes the change and switches off when glucose returns to its set point.","accept":["insulin rises after glucose rises, glucose returns to baseline, then insulin returns to baseline","the response (insulin) reverses the rise in glucose and stops when glucose is back to normal","glucose goes up to 140 and back to 85 as insulin goes up to 62 and back to 9"]}],"sample":"After the meal glucose rises from 85 to 140 mg/dL, and insulin rises with it from 8 to 62 µU/mL. Glucose then falls back to 85 by 180 minutes, and insulin falls back to 9 as glucose returns. The response (insulin and the uptake it causes) opposed the change and switched off once glucose was back near its set point, which is negative feedback."},{"label":"c","prompt":"Glucagon falls after the meal. Using the model, <b>explain</b> why the fall in glucagon helps return blood glucose to its set point.","points":1,"rubric":[{"point":"Explains that glucagon makes the liver break down glycogen and release glucose, so less glucagon means the liver releases less glucose into the blood while glucose is high, helping the level fall.","accept":["lower glucagon means less glycogen breakdown and less glucose released by the liver","the liver stops adding glucose to the blood when glucagon is low","less glucagon removes a source of glucose that would oppose insulin"]}],"sample":"In the model, glucagon makes the liver break down glycogen and release glucose into the blood. When glucose is high after the meal, glucagon falls from 90 to 70 pg/mL, so the liver adds less glucose to the blood. With less glucose coming in while insulin drives glucose out, the level falls back toward its set point faster."},{"label":"d","prompt":"In type 2 diabetes, muscle and fat cells respond weakly to insulin. <b>Predict</b> how the glucose and insulin curves after the same meal would differ from those shown. <b>Justify</b> your prediction using the model.","points":1,"rubric":[{"point":"Predicts that glucose rises higher and stays high longer, and insulin rises higher (and stays higher), because the effectors take up glucose poorly, so the stimulus (high glucose) persists and the beta cells keep releasing insulin.","accept":["glucose peaks higher and falls slowly; insulin is higher because high glucose keeps stimulating the beta cells","higher, longer glucose and higher insulin because the response does not remove the stimulus","both curves end up higher than the healthy ones"]}],"sample":"Glucose would rise higher than 140 mg/dL and take much longer to return to 85, and insulin would rise higher than 62 µU/mL and stay high. In the model, insulin lowers glucose only through the effectors, muscle and fat cells taking up glucose. If they respond weakly, glucose is removed slowly, so the stimulus stays, and the beta cells keep releasing more insulin."}]}